prescan is deleted; guards inside recognition refuse as status -7 `nesting <m> <b> rule|lookahead` before any buffer exists: 8,192 live rule frames (the frame depth is the length of the current context's parent chain, which equals the live rule activations; recorded per context at virtual rule entry), 4,096 SLL lookahead links and 256 full-LL links past the outer context (on every RULE transition of prediction); both walkers are iterative; recognition, export and worker disposal run on a wrapper-owned 16 MiB thread (spec §7.6). Evidence: `bb probe:jolt-j5` on macOS arm64 (the review's repros refusing three times then parsing `x * 6`; at/over per shape exactly at the predicted n; 10x the old crash depths; the old crash depths in children on the jolt thread; a 20,000-deep `1+` tree equal to the JVM's packet), `bb calibrate:jolt-j5` (every bound at most a quarter of the parse stack per shape, worst 1.6 MB), the lifecycle and accessor legs; Linux x86_64 (shiva 2026-10-29), all at the round-3b bytes: `bb probe:jolt-j5` rc 0, `bb test:jolt-j5` ALL PASS 0 FAIL, `bb calibrate:jolt-j5` rc 0 (worst quarter-stack margin unchanged), the J0c legs rc 0 (cycles 20,000 with 16-byte malloc growth, workers, parity zero pins), the sanitizer soak's 50 rounds all pass (its exit-time LeakSanitizer report is the classified P3 row below — suppressed, the corpus reads HARNESS ok), and the leak experiment is cycle-independent (0/5/50/100 cycles all exactly 181,088 bytes in 3,312 allocations) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:63:| jolt Python parser has no deterministic work budget: time inside the input and nesting axes is unbounded (a `1+` chain is quadratic in the pinned runtime; an SLL lookahead over a `1*` chain grows faster; a few-kilobyte source holds the parse thread for minutes), and no refusal is keyed on elapsed time (a refusal is a function of the source) | spec §7.6 | jolt seat | open; slice J5c implemented (2026-11-01): one `work` counter charged at three units — every rule ent